--- Overview
This PR adds git-based bundle versioning and relative symlink support to buildtools. It also makes build specification loading stricter: the --config flag no longer has a default, and unknown YAML fields are rejected.

--- Key Changes
buildtools/git.go
  * New Version type with Short, Build (<short>+<commit>[-dirty]), Commit, BuildTime (UTC) and Dirty, for CFBundleShortVersionString and CFBundleVersion.
  * New Git.Version(ctx, runner, short) returns the HEAD commit and reports whether the working tree has uncommitted changes. The caller decides whether a dirty tree is acceptable.

buildtools/file.go
  * New SymlinkRelative(target, link) Step creates the link using a path relative to the link's directory, via ln -s -f -n. With -n, an existing link to a directory is replaced instead of a new link being created inside that directory.

buildtools/cli.go
  * CommonFlags.Config no longer defaults to spec.yaml. A tool can supply its own defaults by calling Config.SetDefaults before registering flags.
  * Load returns an error if no YAML file is given.
  * Load now parses strictly: misspelt or misplaced fields are errors (previously WithStrictFields(false)).

Tests / docs
  * Added tests for Git.Version (clean, dirty, outside a repo), SymlinkRelative (relative path, replacement, empty args), tool-supplied Config defaults, and strict parsing.
  * Updated README.md to match.

--- Breaking Changes
  * --config no longer defaults to spec.yaml. Tools that relied on this must call Config.SetDefaults, or users must pass --config.
  * YAML build specifications with unknown fields now fail to load instead of having those fields ignored.

